#~
A recovered '?->' value compares the same way inline as it does through a local.

#1067. The chain value a recovering Try() stores is Nil, and comparing it
against Nil gave different answers depending on where the comparison was
written:

  if(a?->Hop() = Nil)        -- wrong once the callee was JIT-compiled
  r := b?->Hop(); if(r = Nil) -- right in every mode

Same program, same build. 'obc --asm' shows both forms emit the same shape
through the same chain temporary, so this was never a miscompile; the two
differ only in that the second copies the temporary into a local before
anything reads it. The failing method is the only thing compiled -- the method
holding the '?->' carries TRY_START, which is in neither backend's whitelist,
so it stays interpreted either way.

NO JIT OPT-OUT, deliberately: the two modes agreeing is the entire property
under test, so a marker here would remove the only thing it checks.

The divide-by-zero is raised inside the CALLEE so the error travels back as a
compiled method's status rather than being raised in the frame that holds the
handler. The warm-up loop uses a PLAIN call -- a '?->' there would push and pop
a handler 500 times and muddy what is being measured.
~#

class Hop {
	@n : Int;

	New(n : Int) {
		@n := n;
	}

	# fails when @n is 0, and the failure is raised here rather than in the
	# caller that holds the handler
	method : public : Self() ~ Hop {
		if(10 / @n > 0) {
			return @self;
		};

		return @self;
	}
}

class TryChainInlineCompare {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		ok := true;

		# warm Self past the JIT threshold with calls that SUCCEED, so the
		# failing call below runs in compiled code in every pass
		good := Hop->New(1);
		warm := true;
		each(i : 500) {
			if(good->Self() = Nil) {
				warm := false;
			};
		};
		ok := Report("a valid call returns its object while warming", warm) & ok;

		# the two forms, on separate objects so neither can affect the other
		a := Hop->New(0);
		inline_is_nil := a?->Self() = Nil;
		ok := Report("a recovered chain compares equal to Nil INLINE", inline_is_nil) & ok;

		b := Hop->New(0);
		through_local := b?->Self();
		ok := Report("a recovered chain compares equal to Nil through a local",
			through_local = Nil) & ok;

		# and the two forms agree, which is the property #1067 broke
		ok := Report("both forms agree", inline_is_nil = (through_local = Nil)) & ok;

		# recovery left the program usable
		ok := Report("a valid call after recovery still works", good?->Self() <> Nil) & ok;

		if(<>ok) {
			Runtime->Exit(1);
		};
	}

	function : Report(name : String, ok : Bool) ~ Bool {
		if(ok) {
			"PASS: {$name}"->PrintLine();
			return true;
		};

		"FAIL: {$name}"->PrintLine();

		return false;
	}
}
